@* What a page frame renders instead of the page when the visitor may not see the store the page would
   be about. Nothing is fetched to draw it, which is the point: the refusal happens before the page
   exists, so no store the visitor may not see is read on their behalf. The store is not named beyond the
   key they already asked for - a refusal that described what is behind it would be the leak the
   per-store policy exists to prevent. *@

<div class="ms-page">
    <div class="ms-empty" role="alert">
        <p class="ms-empty-title">Not authorized</p>
        <p class="ms-empty-description">
            @if (string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(StoreName))
            {
                <text>You are not authorized to view this Marten store.</text>
            }
            else
            {
                <text>You are not authorized to view the Marten store <strong>@StoreName</strong>.</text>
            }
            Pick another one from the store list above.
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// The store key the visitor was pointed at, named so that someone who administers several knows
    /// which one they were refused rather than only that they were.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? StoreName { get; set; }
}
